# Test: a ddos local drop rule does not survive a restart. A ze_ddos-local table
# is planted in the kernel BEFORE ze starts, owned by nobody, and the daemon
# clears it as the ddos-local plugin comes up.
#
# VALIDATES: spec-ddos-timing-leaves-reach-no-worker, the round 4 answer -- a
#            ddos-local drop rule is an attack response and never persistent
#            state. It is not saved across a restart, because a reboot can be
#            exactly how an operator clears state, and protection afterwards
#            comes from the attack being detected again. clearStaleDropRule
#            (internal/plugins/ddos/local/register.go) is what holds it.
# PREVENTS:  a blackhole that outlives the process that installed it. The exit
#            path cannot be relied on for this: ProcessManager.Stop cancels every
#            plugin at once with no dependency order, so the firewall engine can
#            reach CloseBackend while ddos-local is still joining its cap worker,
#            after which ApplyAll writes nothing. `firewall flush-on-shutdown
#            false` leaves the table in place by design, and a crash or a power
#            loss runs no exit path at all. Whatever the previous process did,
#            the next one starts clean.
#
# WHY NOTHING ELSE COULD REMOVE IT. The planted table is owned by nobody in the
# running daemon: no responder claims it, so neither an AttackCleared nor
# max-mitigation-duration can reach it, and this test runs no flood so no
# responder ever installs anything. The firewall engine cannot reach it either.
# shouldDeleteTable (internal/plugins/firewall/nft/backend_linux.go) deletes a
# ze_ table only when the name is in the desired set or in THIS backend
# instance's applied map, and a table a previous process wrote is in neither --
# which is also why FlushAllTables would not clear it. The one-time removal of
# what an older ze build left behind cannot account for it either: that removal
# matches the BARE names those builds wrote (internal/component/firewall/
# legacy_tables.go), and this table carries the ze_ ownership prefix.
#
# WHY COPP IS IN THIS CONFIG. It keeps the firewall engine's lifetime independent
# of ddos-local's, so no firewall-engine shutdown can run while the probe reads
# the kernel back. copp declares firewall as a dependency, as ddos-local does.
#
# WHY THE TABLE AND NOT ONLY THE RULE. The sweep owes both. It claims the table
# name for one reconcile, which is what makes the backend delete what it finds
# under that name, and then withdraws the name so the empty table that reconcile
# created goes as well. An implementation that reconciled once would leave an
# empty ze_ddos-local table behind and this assertion reddens on it.
#
# STATUS: a Linux/QEMU integration test; NOT executable on the darwin dev host.
#   It needs net-admin: the planter writes the nftables table through netlink and
#   the probe reads it back. No bpf family is needed, because no victim prefix has
#   to resolve: this test plants the rule rather than provoking one.
option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin
# Serialize against every other ddos test. They share one network namespace and
# one ze_ddos-local table name, so a sibling's live mitigation is
# indistinguishable from this test's planted one. Rationale in full:
# test/plugin/ddos-direction.ci.
option=exclusive:group=ddos-flood
option=env:var=ze.log.ddos.local:value=info
# 10s for the planter, then daemon startup and the readback.
option=timeout:value=90s

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
ddos {
	local {
		response-level enforce
		max-mitigation-duration 3600
	}
}

control-plane-protection {
	bgp {
		rate 100/second
		burst 20
	}
}

plugin {
	external ddos-stale-table-swept-probe {
		run "ze-test fixture plugin/ddos-stale-table-swept-driver"
		encoder json
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze-test fixture plugin/ddos-stale-table-plant:timeout=15s
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=60s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=STALE-TABLE-PLANTED 127.0.0.13
expect=stderr:contains=STALE-TABLE-SWEPT 127.0.0.13
